**C.** One file, read by the tool: `project-memory/limits.json` (JSON because the tool is stdlib-only and Python's JSON is stdlib; YAML would need PyYAML or a parser; Markdown would need a parser and invites edits). Fields per agent: name, file, limit with unit (`lines`, `utf8_bytes`, `utf16_code_units`, `tokens`), `source` (URL/path + date), `measured: true|false`, and `example: Rd3.1b` when false. Tool computes strictest across rows; when file absent, uses the interim example and prints "interim (Rd3.1b); not measured". Reviewer sees interim stops when every row is measured: true and source is a measurement command/date; the tool prints which row bound.

Should it be AGENTS.md? No: numbers are data with units and sources; AGENTS.md is instructions read at start, and parsing Markdown would make the tool brittle; AGENTS.md can point to the file in one line.

Why not tool constants? The tool is shared by every project; a project fact in shared code (a) can't be right for two projects, (b) turns a measurement into a code edit, and (c) makes upgrade a merge. #12 changing conditions not people: the condition is per project, so per-project file.

