Where does the file live? `project-memory/limits.json` — inside project's memory folder, which is project's own per kit. But careful: the tool's `project-memory/` scan only reads `entries/`; limits.json at project-memory root is not an entry, so no interference. Or repo root `.memory-index.json` keeps it out of the memory folder. Which is better? project-memory is "the project's own"; limits are project config. Root file `.memory-index-limits.json` is hidden and discoverable; project-memory/limits.json sits with the memory it governs. I'd choose `project-memory/limits.json` because the limit is a property of the memory's readers and travels with the memory tree (not the kit), and the tool already knows `project-memory/`. But if a project imports the memory kit into an existing repo, project-memory exists only after adoption; fine. And if the folder is absent, check mode passes "nothing to check" (C1.3), so no problem.

