(2) Measure later, and only on the engines that the project's reader set uses, on the installed versions; never rely on docs as a measurement. Why later not now: no project has adopted project-memory yet (no project-memory/ in this repo); the reader set isn't fixed; measuring all six engines for a design no project uses is effort before value. But not "not at all": the record's own basis says the values come from measuring readers' load paths; until measured, the design runs on a marked example — acceptable as interim, not as the end state. Measure once, record commands+dates in the config, and re-measure when an engine upgrades or a new agent joins. The research already gives the candidates and the conflicts (agy 12k chars vs 24k bytes; Qwen code units vs bytes; Claude 25KB ambiguity) — those are the rows to test first, because they can lower the strictest limit. The agy 20,000-token budget across rules also means a file limit alone doesn't capture the binding constraint; measuring must test the whole start-load, not just one file's bytes. That's a good point: Codex/dsh combined budgets mean measuring a single file's limit is not enough; the measured value depends on the other instruction files. So a measurement protocol per engine: fill the start file (and the other instruction files) and see where truncation starts; record the condition, not just the number.

Now, "now, later, or not at all": Answer: build now-ish (own run), measure later, and not "docs only". Maybe give a condition: if the next project that adopts memory is this one, measure before its first real INDEX; otherwise the example stands. Is measurement on engines needed at all if no agent reads? It's needed once a project relies on the number as a MUST.

